Demo chrome (kurtbruns/demo.getkestrel.dev#9)

What: in the demo's auth mode ("demo", from 0001-demo-auth), the editor's identity chip
reads "Demo sandbox" with no sign-out link, and a strip above the workspace says the
sandbox is private and temporary, with "Reset demo" (a link to the demo Worker's own
confirmation page, /_demo/reset) and a link to getkestrel.dev. The strip's height is
published as --demo-h, which the full-height layout and the sticky sidebar subtract; on a
phone it clears the fixed menu button. Every public page (card pages, the reader shell, a
hosted post page) gets a matching strip pinned to the bottom, with "Open the editor" and
"Reset demo". A client spec covers the editor side.

Why: a visitor should always know they're in a disposable sandbox, and how to start over.
The public strip rides the dev badge's injection points (devDashboardStyle and
devDashboardBadge in src/lib/page.ts), which reach only pages as served, never a frozen
render or a sent email (I3); in this build those helpers always emit the strip, so a
future caller elsewhere would carry it too. Reset is a link, not a form, because a post
page forbids form posts (form-action 'none') and every public page forbids script.

SPEC §11 says no public page links into the Access-gated admin; its one exception is the
dev-only shortcut. The strip's "Open the editor" link departs from that on purpose: in
the demo the editor has no Access wall (0001-demo-auth), and each visitor's editor is
their own sandbox, so the reason for the rule doesn't hold. No I-invariant is touched.

Kestrel's own server specs that assert the dev badge's absence (test/archive.spec.ts)
fail on the patched tree, as its auth specs do under 0001; its client specs pass.

See DESIGN.md, "Static assets and demo chrome" and "The patch set".

